# Line endings
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Git on Windows converts LF to CRLF on checkout by default. For most files that
# is harmless, but a shell script with carriage returns does not run: bash reads
# the \r as part of the command and reports "No such file or directory" for a
# line that is plainly there. Anything executed by a POSIX shell is therefore
# pinned to LF regardless of the platform it is checked out on.

* text=auto

*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# PowerShell is fine with either, but keeping it CRLF matches the platform it
# runs on and avoids a diff the first time somebody edits it in a Windows editor.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Nothing should rewrite these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# Generated and vendored files: collapse them in pull request diffs so a lock
# file change does not bury the code it accompanies.
frontend/package-lock.json linguist-generated=true -diff
backend/requirements.txt linguist-generated=true
backend/requirements-dev.txt linguist-generated=true
